    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 11. Per-Class Precision and Threshold Tuning\n",
    "\n",
    "### What is a decision threshold?\n",
    "\n",
    "When `clf.predict()` classifies a complaint it internally calls `predict_proba()`\n",
    "first, gets a probability for every category, and then picks whichever category\n",
    "has the highest probability. That \"pick the highest\" rule uses an implicit\n",
    "threshold of 0 — it always makes a prediction no matter how uncertain the model is.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Threshold tuning** changes that rule: for each category we set a minimum\n",
    "confidence the model must reach before it will commit to that label. If no\n",
    "category clears its threshold the complaint is flagged as **\"Review needed\"**\n",
    "and routed to a human. This directly trades recall (we label fewer complaints\n",
    "automatically) for precision (the ones we do label are more likely to be correct).\n",
    "\n",
    "This is especially useful in practice when the cost of a wrong label (routing a\n",
    "mortgage complaint to the credit card team) is higher than the cost of deferring\n",
    "to a human reviewer.\n",
    "\n",
